# This repo is LF-canonical. Git already stores every text file with LF endings
# (core.autocrlf normalized them on commit) and CI runs on Linux, so the source-
# slicing drift tests assume LF — e.g. tests slice a function body with
# indexOf('\n}\n') and match regexes with bare \n. A Windows checkout WITHOUT this
# file gets CRLF, the '\n}\n' sentinel never matches '\r\n}\r\n', the slice overruns
# the whole file, and the tests fail on content that isn't theirs. Forcing LF in the
# working tree on every platform keeps the checkout identical to what git stores and
# what CI runs.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Images are binary — never normalize their bytes.
*.png binary
